---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---

<Layout title="Why Cut Paths? — Cutline">
  <h1>Why Cut Paths?</h1>

  <section class="intro">
    <p class="lead">
      Many people believe Narge should not be easy to reach on the ordinary internet. 
      A cut path makes that belief actionable.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>The Problem</h2>
    <p>
      The current internet makes Narge content trivially accessible. A single typo, search result, 
      or link can expose anyone to content from the <a href="/spec">Narge class</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      This is not about prohibition. It's about routing. The internet can work differently.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>This is not a kids product.</strong> It is not family-device scare messaging. 
      It is a technical routing choice for anyone who prefers that Narge not be the default.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What a Cut Path Does</h2>
    <p>
      A cut path is a DNS resolver that does not resolve the Narge content class. It makes Narge 
      harder to reach—not impossible, just not the default.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Sites identified as Narge-purpose return no address (NXDOMAIN)</li>
      <li>Everything else resolves normally</li>
      <li>No accounts, no filtering dashboard, no "family settings"</li>
      <li>This is a technical class, not a moral judgment system</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Why This Approach</h2>
    
    <h3>Narrow and Technical</h3>
    <p>
      Narge is a defined content class. See the <a href="/spec">specification</a> for the full definition. 
      It is not "objectionable content" or "adult material." The definition is narrow by design.
    </p>

    <h3>Host-Level Intent</h3>
    <p>
      The classification looks at a site's purpose, not individual pictures. The <a href="/spec">spec</a> 
      defines what is and is not Narge based on purpose.
    </p>

    <h3>Open and Verifiable</h3>
    <p>
      The specification, block list source, and resolver configuration are all public. You can read 
      exactly what is happening. No company stamp, no secret rules.
    </p>

    <h3>Not a Complete Filter</h3>
    <p>
      This is not a kids-safety product, an addiction treatment, or a house filter. If you need those, 
      use a complete filtering solution. A cut path addresses one narrow class.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Not Government, Not a Brand</h2>
    <p>
      This is not a law. It's not a government program. It's not a certification or trust seal. 
      It's infrastructure anyone can run.
    </p>
    <p>
      The goal: make it normal for ISPs and carriers to offer a cut path option. Show demand 
      with usage numbers. Let users choose their own routing.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Who This Is For</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Parents</strong> who want baseline protection without full filtering</li>
      <li><strong>Individuals</strong> who prefer a default path that doesn't serve Narge</li>
      <li><strong>Organizations</strong> that want a secular, technical option</li>
      <li><strong>Anyone</strong> who believes Narge should not be the default internet</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      This is not a religious product. It's not a political statement. It's a routing choice.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Honesty About Limitations</h2>
    <p>
      A cut path will not stop someone who wants to find Narge. It makes it harder to reach by accident 
      or habit. If you need stronger protection, use additional tools.
    </p>
    <p>
      The hostname list is an approximation maintained at 
      <code>dist/narge.txt</code> in the public repository. 
      Some sites will be misclassified. That is expected.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="cta">
    <h2>Using Narge</h2>
    <p>
      The Narge hostname list is publicly available. Cutline is one consumer. 
      See <a href="/get-on">Get On</a> if you want to use Cutline as your resolver.
    </p>
  </section>
</Layout>

<style>
  .intro {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  .lead {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.7;
    color: var(--color-text);
  }

  section {
    margin: var(--spacing-2xl) 0;
  }

  h3 {
    margin-top: var(--spacing-xl);
  }

  ul {
    padding-left: var(--spacing-lg);
    margin: var(--spacing-md) 0;
  }

  li {
    margin-bottom: var(--spacing-sm);
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .cta {
    background: var(--color-card-bg);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--spacing-xl);
    margin-top: var(--spacing-2xl);
  }

  .cta h2 {
    margin-top: 0;
  }

  .cta p {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }
</style>
